NAJA HATCH 0.0.1 experimental Rust seed

Usage:
  naja --version
  naja doctor [--json] [--runner direct|wsl]
  naja init NEW_DIRECTORY
  naja lex SOURCE
  naja check SOURCE [--json] [--project-root PATH]
  naja build SOURCE --target x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu --output NEW_FILE [--project-root PATH]
  naja run SOURCE [--runner wsl] [--timeout-ms N] [--project-root PATH] [-- ARGS...]
  naja test DIRECTORY|SOURCE [--runner wsl] [--filter TEXT] [--json] [--timeout-ms N] [--project-root PATH]

Use naja COMMAND --help for command options and limits.
This seed supports native arbitrary-precision decimal integers, text, bytes, booleans and None; +, -, *, //, %, comparisons/chains, not/and/or, assignment, truthy if/else/while, assertions, print, len(text/bytes/tuple/list) and Text/Bytes/Tuple/List[index].
Negative indices count from the end; text indexing returns one Unicode scalar and bytes indexing returns Int. Bounds report NJA207 and exit 107; slices remain unsupported.
Parenthesized tuples support up to 128 elements, len, truth and indexing; (value) remains grouping and (value,) is a singleton. Tuple display/comparison/numeric operations report type exit 105.
Lists use [elements] with at most 128 entries, len/truth/index and shared indexed mutation. Assignment evaluates value, sequence, index once in that order. List display/comparison/numeric operations report type exit 105; resizing remains unsupported.
The hosted arena is limited to 64 MiB; exhaustion reports NJA203 and exits 103.
General division work is bounded to 1000000 numerator/divisor limb pairs; exceeding it exits 103. Division by zero reports NJA206 and exits 106.
Explicit from naja.core import i64 selects the separate checked-i64 slice; mixed domains remain unsupported.
.naja selects edition 2026.1; .py keeps its separate compatibility dialect.
--project-root PATH explicitly activates the owning .naja project alias table; no ambient discovery or .py alias activation.
First-order top-level functions support plain positional parameters, self/earlier calls, return/fallthrough None and isolated local frames. Calls are bounded to depth 256 and 2 MiB source stack (4 KiB helper reserve); ordinary OS stack availability is required.
Globals/closures/defaults/kwargs/function values, floats, full collection semantics, classes, complete modules/types, full grammar and self-hosting remain unimplemented.
